    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d54">

                <pb facs="0171" n="127" sameAs="frus:frus1964-68v06/pg127"/>

                <head>54. Memorandum From Robert N. Ginsburgh of the National Security Council Staff
                    to the President’s Special Assistant (Rostow)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d54-f1"
                    /></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1968-02-04">February 4,
                        1968</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>I would speculate that there are two possible reasons why the attack on Khe Sanh
                    has not yet materialized: <list>
                        <item>—Our B–52 and tactical air attacks may have upset their
                            timing—especially if the air bombardment actually put their headquarters
                            out of operation for a day or so.</item>
                        <item>—They may have planned the attack on Khe Sanh to coincide with a
                            second round of attacks on the cities. Initial attacks on the cities
                            would be designed to force General Westmoreland to commit his reserves.
                            The second round would keep them committed while they launched a major
                            assault on Khe Sanh.</item>
                    </list></p>
                <pb facs="0172" n="128" xml:id="pg128"/>
                <p>Since the first attacks did not achieve their objectives, it is conceivable that
                    the attack might not take place.</p>
                <p>More likely, the enemy would try to carry out its original plan. If so, we might
                    expect the battle for Khe Sanh to start within the next three days. Various
                    intelligence reports indicate; for example: <list>
                        <item>—Attack as early as possible before 5 February.<anchor n="2"
                                xml:id="d54-f2"/></item>
                        <item>—General Loan, police director, believes another attack on Saigon is
                            scheduled for 4 or 5 February.</item>
                        <item>—Enemy troop movements in Pleiku area indicate possibility of attack
                            the night of 4 February (today our time).</item>
                        <item>—A second attack is scheduled for Nha Trang ten days after the first
                            attack (6 or 7 February).</item>
                        <item>—Special communications plans for enemy units the night of 4–5
                            February.</item>
                    </list></p>
                <closer><signed>G</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d54-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d54-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Johnson Library, National Security File, Country File, Vietnam, 2 A (2),
                    I Corps and DMZ, 2/68 [2 of 2]. Secret.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d54-f2" n="2" xml:id="d54-f2-content">
                <p>Late on the night of February 4–5 enemy shelling and minor ground assaults on Khe
                    Sanh began but were quickly beaten back.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
